# Admin editing ethos

Any time we add or change how Mark edits the storefront, design it so he can
**see the site and poke it** — not fill out a spreadsheet of forms.

## The goal

Edits should feel like rearranging the shop floor. If a visitor can see it on
`/`, Mark should be able to change it from a screen that **looks like `/`**,
with obvious click / drag / pencil controls.

## What that looks like (x, y, z)

1. **Mirror the public layout.** `/admin/homepage` (and similar) should match
   the real homepage sections, spacing, and cards. Public `/` stays clean —
   no pencils, no drag handles.
2. **Cyan = “this is editable / selected / being dragged.”** Use the existing
   teal/cyan accent (`#14B8A6` / `#00F2FE`) as the hover and active border,
   never a separate “admin purple” look.
3. **Pencil on every editable unit.** Categories, reviews, handiwork cards,
   sold thumbnails — each gets a small pencil. Pencil deepens into that unit’s
   world; drag reorders peers at the current level.
4. **Drag to reorder, don’t type a sort number.** Persist order as `sort_order`.
5. **Click a piece → edit every stored field.** Title, price, category, kind,
   description, tags, specs, photos, sold/featured. No hidden columns.
6. **Category pencil → shop-like page for that category.** Cover photo, all
   pieces in the category, drag-reorder pieces. Same visual language as `/shop`.
7. **Short content edits in place.** Reviews (quote, author, stars) open a
   small panel on the mirror page — don’t force a full route for three fields.
8. **Add with `+`, remove without surprise.** Removing from “Available
   Handiworks” un-features a piece; marking Sold moves it to the sold strip and
   off the shop grid. Deleting a category or review asks for confirmation.

## When adding a new feature

Ask: “Can Mark discover this by looking at the page and clicking?” If the only
answer is a buried settings form or a raw slug field, redesign before shipping.

Prefer: mirror page → pencil / drag → focused editor.
Avoid: admin-only tables of IDs, manual `sort_order` number inputs, content
hardcoded in JSX.
